orithm to $A.$\n\n**Step 1.** From the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-7\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=7.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda =-7.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-(-7)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 12 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 12 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-6{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-6{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have two linearly independent eigenvectors\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda =7.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking for nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)x=0,$ we obtain the eigenvector \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the invertible matrix $P$ using our eigenvectors, as follows:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186437106 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186437108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437106 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b334aaf-b9e6-5e6d-8178-ec66ebfce8bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437106 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186437105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437107 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186437106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437107 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "930f64a0-82cd-5548-be71-7e0d4be897ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437107 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186404926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437108 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186437109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437108 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ad638d86-1fb6-559c-aec0-5ebfd955bb6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437108 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186272047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437109 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186437110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437109 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d1a69423-dea9-5f61-a4af-15bcb3704ae7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437109 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186272048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437110 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "debc197f-086e-55eb-b1ea-1023d09ebd2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437110 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186437104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485750 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186486466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485750 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186486473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485750 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186486483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485750 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186486495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485750 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186486513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485750 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921864